#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:9
msgid "Ensuring security across machines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:11
msgid "**Goal:** Make two different machines communicate securely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:13
msgid "**Tutorial level:** Advanced"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:15
msgid "**Time:** 5 minutes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:20
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:23
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:25
msgid ""
"Before proceeding ensure you have completed the :doc:`Introducing-ros2-"
"security` tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:27
msgid ""
"The previous tutorials have used two ROS nodes on the same machine sending "
"all network communications over the localhost interface. Let's extend that "
"scenario to involve multiple machines, since the benefits of authentication "
"and encryption then become more obvious."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:30
msgid ""
"Suppose that the machine with the keystore created in the previous demo has "
"a hostname ``Alice``, and that we want to also use another machine with "
"hostname ``Bob`` for our multi-machine ``talker/listener`` demo. We need to "
"move some keys from ``Alice`` to ``Bob`` to allow SROS 2 to authenticate and "
"encrypt the transmissions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:35
msgid "Create the second keystore"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:37
msgid ""
"Begin by creating an empty keystore on ``Bob``; the keystore is actually "
"just an empty directory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:41
#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:74
msgid "Linux"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:43
#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:51
msgid ""
"$ ssh Bob\n"
"$ mkdir ~/sros2_demo\n"
"$ exit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:49
#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:81
msgid "MacOS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:57
#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:88
msgid "Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:59
msgid ""
"$ ssh Bob\n"
"$ md C:\\dev\\ros2\\sros2_demo\n"
"$ exit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:67
msgid "Copy files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:69
msgid ""
"Next copy the keys and certificates for the ``talker`` program from "
"``Alice`` to ``Bob``. Since the keys are just text files, we can use ``scp`` "
"to copy them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:76
#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:83
msgid ""
"$ cd ~/sros2_demo/demo_keystore\n"
"$ scp -r talker USERNAME@Bob:~/sros2_demo/demo_keystore"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:90
msgid ""
"$ cd C:\\dev\\ros2\\sros2_demo\\demo_keystore\n"
"$ scp -r talker USERNAME@Bob:/dev/ros2/sros2_demo/demo_keystore"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:97
msgid ""
"Note that in this case the entire keystore is shared across the different "
"machines which may not be the desired behavior, as it may result in a "
"security risk. Please refer to :doc:`../../Build/Deployment-Guidelines/"
"Deployment-Guidelines` for more information in this regard."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:100
msgid ""
"That will be very quick, since it's just copying some very small text files. "
"Now, we're ready to run a multi-machine talker/listener demo!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:105
msgid "Launch the nodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:107
msgid "Once the environment is set up, run the talker on ``Bob``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:109
msgid ""
"$ ros2 run demo_nodes_cpp talker --ros-args --enclave /talker_listener/talker"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:113
msgid "and launch the listener on ``Alice``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:115
msgid ""
"$ ros2 run demo_nodes_py listener --ros-args --enclave /talker_listener/"
"listener"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:119
msgid "Alice will now be receiving encrypted messages from Bob."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Introspection-and-analysis/Security/Security-on-Two.rst:121
msgid ""
"With two machines successfully communicating using both encryption and "
"authentication, you can use the same procedure to add more machines to your "
"ROS graph."
msgstr ""
